A solution forms when both the solute and the solvent mix. However, mixing will only be successful if the compounds in the mixture have the same polarity. As an example, water (\ce{H2O}) is a polar molecule, and methanol (\ce{CH3-OH}) is polar too. Hence, they will be miscible and will form a solution. If the compounds in a mixture have different polarity they will be immiscible. An example is benzene (\ce{C6H6}, nonpolar) and water, or oil (nonpolar) and water (polar). 